Yeah, a fighting mechanic could score well, but only if it feels like a designed combat system, not just “press button, play swing animation, deal damage.”

Based on the brief, they are marking a single Unreal C++ gameplay mechanic, not a full game. They care about whether it is cohesive, refined, enjoyable, implemented in Unreal/C++, and explained well in the video. The PDF says you are assessed on how well the mechanic works together as a cohesive system, and that visual quality is not the main thing.
My honest mark estimate

For your 2D-HD hybrid game, a combat mechanic with:

    swing/attack system

    block

    timed parry

    enemy lock-on

    enemy that can attack back

would probably sit around:

60–69% if it works consistently and feels decent.
That matches the “very good” band: coherent, consistent, good polish, plays fluently.

70–85% if you implement it cleanly in C++ with proper states, timing windows, feedback, enemy behaviour, and refinement.
The rubric specifically rewards “sophisticated” mechanics, good polish, future-proof code, UProperties, finite states/actions, and feedback.

86%+ is possible, but only if the combat feels creative and polished rather than generic. The top band wants a “highly creative response” with sophisticated and polished mechanics.

So: basic sword swing + block + parry = maybe 55–65.
Well-engineered combat loop with lock-on, enemy tells, stamina, posture, timing windows, hit reactions, and clean C++ state logic = 70–85+.
What I’d add to push it higher

The best thing you can do is make the combat about timing and readable decision-making.

Add a combat state machine. Something like:

Idle → Windup → ActiveAttack → Recovery → Block → ParryWindow → Staggered → HitReact → Dead

This is exactly the kind of thing you can talk about in the video: why certain actions lock the player, why parry only works during a small timing window, why recovery prevents spam, and how the system is extensible.

Add enemy telegraphs. Before the enemy attacks, they should show a warning: slight flash, wind-up pose, sound cue, or overhead icon. This makes parry skill-based instead of random.

Add a parry reward. A successful parry should do something meaningful:

    stagger enemy

    open them to a counterattack

    refill stamina

    slow time briefly

    knock the enemy back

    play strong hit-stop/flash feedback

Add stamina or posture. This would make block/parry more interesting. Blocking drains stamina, parrying costs little or restores stamina, attacking costs stamina, getting posture-broken leaves you vulnerable.

Add directional facing/lock-on rules for the 2D-HD setup. Since your game is a hybrid 2D/3D thing, you need to make it clear how the player targets enemies. Auto-lock would be good, but make it explainable:

    detect nearest enemy in front of player

    rotate/focus the player toward target

    keep combat constrained on your 2D plane

    show a simple lock-on marker

    allow switching target if multiple enemies exist

Add hitboxes instead of just distance checks. For a higher mark, have an attack trace or collision box that only becomes active during the attack’s active frames. Then you can show in the code that the sword only damages during the correct part of the swing.

Add combat feedback. This matters a lot because the rubric uses words like polish, fluency, and player enjoyment. Add:

    hit-stop for 0.05–0.1 seconds

    camera shake on hit/parry

    spark effect on block/parry

    sound effects

    enemy knockback

    damage numbers or health bar

    clear “parry success” flash

The mechanic I’d pitch

I’d frame it like this:

“A timing-based 2D-HD melee combat mechanic where the player uses attacks, blocks, and precision parries against enemies with readable attack tells. The system uses C++ combat states, active hitbox windows, stamina/posture management, and an optional lock-on system to make combat readable in a hybrid 2D/3D space.”

That sounds much stronger than “I made sword fighting.”
Minimum version to build

Make this first:

    Player can light attack.

    Attack has windup, active frames, and recovery.

    Enemy can be hit and staggered.

    Enemy attacks the player with a visible windup.

    Player can block to reduce/nullify damage.

    Player can parry only within a short timing window.

    Successful parry staggers enemy and enables a counterattack.

    Add lock-on to nearest enemy.

    Add clear feedback: sound, flash, camera shake, hit-stop.

That would already be a strong mechanic.
What to show in the video

The PDF says your video should include gameplay footage, code overview, input handling, GitHub usage, and project organisation. It specifically asks you to explain refinements, future-proofing, input rules, and what the player can/cannot do at certain stages.

So in your video, don’t just show it working. Say things like:

    “The player cannot parry during attack recovery because the combat state machine blocks defensive actions until recovery ends.”

    “The parry window is exposed as a UPROPERTY, so I can tune it without rewriting the system.”

    “The enemy attack uses a wind-up state before the damage window, giving the player a fair reaction opportunity.”

    “The lock-on system helps the hybrid 2D/3D camera because the player always understands which enemy their attacks will target.”

That is the kind of explanation that hits the programming and technical discussion marks.
My recommendation

Do the fighting mechanic, but don’t make it a generic hack-and-slash. Make it a timing-based duel mechanic.

Your strongest feature list should be:

    C++ combat state machine

    light attack with active frames

    block

    perfect parry window

    enemy telegraphed attacks

    stagger/counterattack system

    stamina or posture

    auto-lock target system

    strong feedback: hit-stop, camera shake, sparks, sound

    clean README and regular GitHub commits

That gives you a realistic shot at 70%+, and if it feels polished and you explain the engineering properly, it could go higher.

