Yes — 8 days is enough, especially because your character controller and inventory are already set up.

But you need to cut the scope hard. Don’t try to make a full combat game. The brief literally says you are not expected to create a game, you are expected to create and refine a single gameplay mechanic.

Your goal should be:

One polished combat encounter. One enemy. One weapon. Clear attack/block/parry loop. Good explanation.

That can score well because the rubric rewards a mechanic that is coherent, consistent, polished, and fluent, not a huge game.
What you can realistically build in 8 days
Must-have system

Build this:

    Player light attack

        Press attack.

        Player enters attack state.

        Hitbox/trace activates only during active frames.

        Enemy takes damage/stagger.

    Block

        Hold block.

        Reduces or prevents damage.

        Maybe drains stamina.

    Parry

        Tap parry/block at the correct time.

        Small parry window, like 0.25s.

        Enemy gets stunned if timed right.

    One enemy

        Enemy walks/ranges toward player.

        Enemy does a telegraphed attack.

        Player can block/parry it.

    Simple lock-on

        Auto-face nearest enemy.

        Show a marker or outline.

        Keep it simple. Don’t make target switching unless you have extra time.

    Feedback

        Hit flash.

        Parry spark/sound.

        Tiny hit-stop.

        Camera shake if easy.

        Health bars or debug text.

That is enough.
What I would not do

Do not add loads of weapons.

Do not make a full enemy AI system.

Do not make combos unless the core mechanic works first.

Do not add multiple enemy types.

Do not rely heavily on inventory unless it supports the mechanic. Inventory can just be “equipped weapon affects damage/parry timing” if you want to mention it.
Best 8-day plan
Day 1 — Combat state machine

Make the player have states:

Idle
Attacking
Blocking
Parrying
HitReact
Stunned/Dead

This is important because the rubric specifically values advanced concepts like actions, finite states, and feedback for higher marks.
Day 2 — Player attack

Add one light attack. Use a sphere/box trace in front of the player, or a hitbox component that activates during the swing.

Minimum polish:

Windup → Active frames → Recovery

Day 3 — Enemy health + hit reaction

Enemy can:

Take damage
Flash red
Stagger backwards
Die or disable

Day 4 — Enemy attack

Enemy should have:

Idle → Chase/Face Player → Windup → Attack Active → Recovery

Add a visible tell before the attack. This makes the parry fair.
Day 5 — Block + parry

Block first. Then parry.

Rules:

Holding block = safe but costs stamina
Perfect parry = short timing window, stuns enemy
Failed parry = player vulnerable

Day 6 — Lock-on + 2D-HD polish

Because your game is hybrid 2D/3D, make the targeting readable:

Find nearest enemy
Face/aim toward that enemy
Show lock-on marker
Keep attacks constrained to your combat plane

This gives you something good to talk about: how you adapted melee combat for a 2D-HD game.
Day 7 — Polish + bugs

Add:

Parry effect
Hit-stop
Sound
Camera shake
Health/stamina UI
Debug text showing current combat state

Debug text is actually useful for the video too.
Day 8 — README, GitHub, video

Do not leave the video until last-last. The submission wants gameplay footage, code overview, input handling, GitHub, and project organisation.

Also, GitHub matters. It is worth 10%, and they specifically don’t want a single-push repo. The PDF says your GitHub should show development over time.
Expected mark if you do this

With 8 days:

Basic working version: 50–59%
Attack, block, enemy damage, mostly works.

Good version: 60–69%
Attack, block, parry, enemy telegraph, lock-on, decent feedback, clean enough code.

Strong version: 70–85%
State machine, exposed UPROPERTY values, clean C++ components, clear input rules, active hit frames, enemy stun/counter window, polish, good video explanation.

I would aim for 60–69 minimum, with a realistic push toward 70+.
The safest scope

Your final mechanic should be:

    A timing-based melee combat mechanic for a 2D-HD Unreal game, featuring light attacks, blocking, precision parries, enemy telegraphs, stun/counter windows, and simple auto-lock targeting.

That is absolutely doable in 8 days.

My advice: start with attack → enemy damage → enemy attack → block → parry, in that order. Don’t touch lock-on until the combat loop works.

