Yes — honestly, for your deadline, that’s probably the best way to do it.

You can keep the character mostly on idle/movement sprites and make the attack readable through a separate sword/slash animation around the player, like your sketch.

For your assessment, this is fine because the PDF says you are not marked on visual quality, and a basic prototype is acceptable as long as the mechanic is cohesive and works properly.

The setup would be:

Player sprite stays mostly idle
↓
Press attack
↓
Spawn/enable sword arc object around player
↓
Sword arc animates through 3–5 frames or rotates around player
↓
Hitbox is active only during the middle of the swing
↓
Enemy takes damage/stagger

So instead of needing full character attack sprites, you only need:

Idle / Move sprites
Sword slash VFX
Attack hitbox
Combat state timing

That is much more realistic.
Best way to do it

Make the sword/slash a separate PaperSpriteComponent or actor attached to the player.

For example:

Player
├── Character Sprite
├── SwordSlashSprite
└── AttackHitbox

During idle:

SwordSlashSprite hidden
AttackHitbox disabled

When attacking:

SwordSlashSprite visible
Play slash animation / rotate slash around player
Enable hitbox during active frames
Disable hitbox after active frames
Return to idle

For directions

You can have one slash animation and just rotate/flip it depending on facing:

Facing Right  = slash appears to the right
Facing Left   = flip X / rotate 180
Facing Up     = slash above player
Facing Down   = slash below player

For 2D-HD, this is actually clean because the sword arc makes the attack direction obvious even if the character sprite barely changes.
What to make visually

Don’t overcomplicate it. Make a simple slash sprite sheet:

Frame 1: small faint arc
Frame 2: bigger arc
Frame 3: full bright arc
Frame 4: fading arc

Each frame can be something like 64x64 or 96x96, separate from your 32x64 character.

The character stays 32x64, but the attack effect can be bigger because it surrounds the character.
Why this is better for marks

This lets you focus on the part that actually matters:

    attack state

    attack windup

    active hit window

    recovery

    hit detection

    block/parry interaction

    enemy reaction

    feedback

That’s much stronger than spending 2 days fighting sprite sheets.

In your video you can literally say:

    “To keep the 2D-HD character readable without relying on complex full-body sprite animation, I separated the combat feedback into a sword arc component and a timed attack hitbox. The character enters an attacking state, the slash visual plays, and the damage box only becomes active during the active frames.”

That sounds engineered, not lazy.
